<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Greece and the Aegean</p><h1 class="st-title">Chalcedon</h1><p class="st-meta">685 BCE – 74 BCE (approx.) · capital Chalcedon</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Megarian colony on the Asian shore of the Bosporus, mocked as the 'city of the blind' for overlooking the better site of Byzantium opposite.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Settlers from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/megara">Megara</a> founded Chalcedon c. 685 BCE on the Asian side of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/bosporan-kingdom">Bosporus</a>, opposite the later site of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/byzantium">Byzantium</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> reports the Persian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/megabazus">Megabazus</a> calling its founders blind for choosing the poorer site when the better one lay across the strait.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Chalcedon lived from farming, fishing and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/bosporan-kingdom">Bosporus</a> traffic, though <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/byzantium">Byzantium</a> controlled the main currents. It came under the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a>, then paid tribute in the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/delian-league">Delian League</a>. The sophist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrasymachus">Thrasymachus</a> came from Chalcedon. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hellenistic-period">Hellenistic period</a> it was closely tied to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a> and pressed by the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-bithynia">Kingdom of Bithynia</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicomedes-iv">Nicomedes IV</a> left <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-bithynia">Bithynia</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 74 BCE, Chalcedon passed under Roman rule; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mithridates-vi-of-pontus">Mithridates</a> defeated a Roman force there that year. It later hosted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-chalcedon">Council of Chalcedon</a> of 451 CE and is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kad-koy">Kadıköy</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/istanbul">Istanbul</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/chalcedon.jpg" alt="Chalcedon" width="320" height="451"><figcaption>Image: QuartierLatin1968 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sancte_Euphemia_Kad%C4%B1k%C3%B6y.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>685 BCE – 74 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/7th-century-bce">7th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Chalcedon</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Greek religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Doric Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kalchedon, Kadikoy</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-380&amp;focus=chalcedon" aria-label="Chalcedon on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5390 715 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Chalcedon lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5710 971L6140 1251M5710 971L5591 1068M5710 971L5304 942"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6140 1251h0M5591 1068h0M5304 942h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5710 971h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5710 971h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=-380&amp;focus=chalcedon">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

